        <section id="supply-chain" class="scroll-mt-20">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-cyan-500 pl-4 mb-6">
                <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-cyan-600">Section 02 • Semiconductor & Geopolitical Bottlenecks</span>
                <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-1">Advanced Packaging, HBM Oligopolies & Resource Weaponization</h2>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-4xl mb-8">
                AI chip deployment is capped not by theoretical logic design, but by physical back-end packaging capacity—specifically TSMC’s Chip-on-Wafer-on-Substrate (CoWoS) technology—and High-Bandwidth Memory (HBM) supply. Simultaneously, US cloud export restrictions and Chinese export bans on gallium, germanium, and antimony expose the entire hardware chain to sudden geopolitical shocks.
            </p>

            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
                <div class="card-elevated p-6 flex flex-col justify-between">
                    <div>
                        <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">Global CoWoS Packaging Demand Surge</h3>
                            <span class="text-xs font-semibold px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-100 text-amber-800">Sold Out Through 2026</span>
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-4">
                            Annual CoWoS wafer demand is surging from ~370k in 2024 to nearly <strong>1,000,000 wafers in 2026</strong>. Nvidia alone has pre-booked over 50% of TSMC's expansion ($105B in supply obligations), driving 10%–20% packaging price hikes.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="chart-container">
                        <canvas id="cowosDemandChart"></canvas>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-elevated p-6 flex flex-col justify-between">
                    <div>
                        <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">High-Bandwidth Memory (HBM) Market Share</h3>
                            <span class="text-xs font-semibold px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 text-blue-800">3-Firm Oligopoly</span>
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-4">
                            SK Hynix commands <strong>50% of HBM revenue</strong> via tight HBM4 integration with Nvidia. Because HBM consumes far more wafer area than standard memory, conventional DRAM supply growth is squeezed to just 16% in 2026, inflating hardware costs industry-wide.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="chart-container">
                        <canvas id="hbmShareChart"></canvas>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="card-elevated p-6 mt-8">
                <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Geopolitical Chokepoints & Foundry Lithography Divergence</h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-6">
                    From raw critical minerals in China to Dutch lithography monopolies and Taiwanese packaging, AI silicon traverses a fragile cross-border pipeline. Meanwhile, leading foundries have split on adopting ASML's $350M+ High-NA EUV machines.
                </p>

                <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 items-stretch mb-8">
                    <div class="bg-rose-50 border-2 border-rose-200 rounded-xl p-4 relative">
                        <div class="text-xs font-extrabold uppercase text-rose-700 mb-1">Stage 1 • Raw Inputs</div>
                        <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base mb-2">China Mineral Controls</div>
                        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
                            Export restrictions on <strong>Gallium, Germanium & Antimony</strong> threaten global substrate, power electronics, and fiber-optic manufacturing.
                        </p>
                        <div class="hidden md:block absolute -right-3 top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 z-10 bg-slate-900 text-white w-6 h-6 rounded-full text-center leading-6 text-xs font-bold">→</div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="bg-blue-50 border-2 border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 relative">
                        <div class="text-xs font-extrabold uppercase text-blue-700 mb-1">Stage 2 • Lithography</div>
                        <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base mb-2">ASML EUV Monopoly</div>
                        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
                            <strong>Intel</strong> deployed High-NA EUV at 18A/14A (2026) to reclaim leadership; <strong>TSMC & Samsung</strong> defer High-NA until ~2030 to protect margins.
                        </p>
                        <div class="hidden md:block absolute -right-3 top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 z-10 bg-slate-900 text-white w-6 h-6 rounded-full text-center leading-6 text-xs font-bold">→</div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="bg-amber-50 border-2 border-amber-200 rounded-xl p-4 relative">
                        <div class="text-xs font-extrabold uppercase text-amber-700 mb-1">Stage 3 • Integration</div>
                        <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base mb-2">Taiwan & Korea Bottleneck</div>
                        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
                            TSMC <strong>CoWoS</strong> (52–78 wk lead times) + South Korean <strong>HBM4</strong> stacks (+70% price premium over HBM3E) govern total GPU output.
                        </p>
                        <div class="hidden md:block absolute -right-3 top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 z-10 bg-slate-900 text-white w-6 h-6 rounded-full text-center leading-6 text-xs font-bold">→</div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="bg-slate-100 border-2 border-slate-300 rounded-xl p-4">
                        <div class="text-xs font-extrabold uppercase text-slate-700 mb-1">Stage 4 • Compute Access</div>
                        <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-base mb-2">US BIS Cloud Controls</div>
                        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
                            US export controls expand beyond physical chips to restrict foreign access to advanced AI training clusters via US cloud providers.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="energy-crisis" class="scroll-mt-20">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-6">
                <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-amber-600">Section 03 • Electrical Grid & Infrastructure</span>
                <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-1">The "Speed-to-Power" Crisis & Behind-the-Meter Generation</h2>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-4xl mb-8">
                Modern AI server racks draw up to <strong>120 kW</strong>—more than ten times legacy 5–10 kW racks. After decades of flat electricity growth, US and European grids face severe transmission backlogs, forcing hyperscalers to abandon traditional utility timelines in favor of "Bring Your Own Power" (BYOP) nuclear, geothermal, and fuel cell solutions.
            </p>

            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
                <div class="card-elevated p-6 flex flex-col justify-between">
                    <div>
                        <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">Data Center Electricity Surge (2024 vs. 2030)</h3>
                            <span class="text-xs font-semibold px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-100 text-amber-800">IEA Projections (TWh)</span>
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-4">
                            Global data center power demand will more than double to <strong>945 TWh by 2030</strong>. The US faces a +240 TWh (+130%) surge, pushing per-capita data center power use above 1,200 kWh/year—roughly 10% of an average US household's total electricity.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="chart-container">
                        <canvas id="energyDemandChart"></canvas>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-elevated p-6 flex flex-col justify-between">
                    <div>
                        <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">US Grid Interconnection Queue Crisis</h3>
                            <span class="text-xs font-semibold px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-rose-100 text-rose-800">Up to 7-Year Wait Times</span>
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-4">
                            In Texas (ERCOT), large-load requests hit <strong>466.5 GW</strong> (90.2% of the entire queue). In PJM (Northern Virginia), >70 GW of data center demand caused wholesale capacity costs to spike to $114.50/MWh, triggering regulatory pushback.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="chart-container">
                        <canvas id="gridQueueChart"></canvas>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mt-8">
                <div class="card-elevated p-6 border-t-4 border-emerald-500">
                    <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
                        <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-700">BYOP Pillar 1 • Geothermal</span>
                        <span class="text-xl">🌋</span>
                    </div>
                    <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Enhanced Geothermal (EGS)</h4>
                    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                        <strong>Fervo Energy</strong> applies horizontal shale-drilling tech to deliver 24/7 carbon-free firm power, backed by a landmark <strong>10.5 GW agreement with Microsoft</strong> and a 396 MW project with Google.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-elevated p-6 border-t-4 border-blue-600">
                    <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
                        <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-blue-700">BYOP Pillar 2 • Nuclear</span>
                        <span class="text-xl">⚛️</span>
                    </div>
                    <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Nuclear SMRs & Restarts</h4>
                    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                        Hyperscalers have committed to <strong>over 10 GW of nuclear capacity</strong>, funding Small Modular Reactors (SMRs) and signing exclusive PPAs to restart or monopolize legacy gigawatt fission plants.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-elevated p-6 border-t-4 border-amber-500">
                    <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
                        <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-700">BYOP Pillar 3 • Onsite Rapid</span>
                        <span class="text-xl">⚡</span>
                    </div>
                    <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Solid Oxide Fuel Cells</h4>
                    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                        <strong>Bloom Energy</strong> deploys modular fuel cells running on natural gas or hydrogen in months rather than years, bypassing multi-year high-voltage transformer and turbine backlogs.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="capex-paradox" class="scroll-mt-20">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-rose-600 pl-4 mb-6">
                <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-rose-600">Section 04 • Financial Sustainability & Credit Risk</span>
                <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mt-1">The $600B Revenue Gap & Private Credit Bridge</h2>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-4xl mb-8">
                Global AI capital expenditure is projected to surpass <strong>$1 trillion annually by 2026</strong> ($1.8T cumulative), yet only <strong>26% of enterprises</strong> have successfully moved generative AI pilots into profit-generating production. To bridge this massive ROI gap without exhausting corporate balance sheets, hyperscalers are turning to an $800 billion private credit and Asset-Backed Securities (ABS) boom.
            </p>

            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
                <div class="card-elevated p-6 flex flex-col justify-between">
                    <div>
                        <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
                            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900">Infrastructure Spend vs. Monetization Reality</h3>
                            <span class="text-xs font-semibold px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-rose-100 text-rose-800">Sequoia & Goldman Data</span>
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-4">
                            To earn healthy margins on $1T+ annual hardware and data center capex, the software layer must generate <strong>$600B/year</strong>. Currently, 74% of piloting firms remain trapped in the "valley of death" due to 4x–7x hidden deployment costs.
